        "explanation": "Both images are WGCNA module-trait heatmaps of the same type. Each has a single Disease column, module color bars on the left, and correlation values printed in each cell. The format follows the guideline, but the data are quite different.\n\n**Reference:** It shows 10 module eigengenes: red, brown, blue, yellow, magenta, pink, black, green, turquoise and grey. Their correlations with Disease run from -0.25 to 0.34:\n- red 0.21\n- brown -0.082\n- blue 0.2\n- yellow 0.34\n- magenta 0.076\n- pink 0.27\n- black -0.21\n- green -0.25\n- turquoise -0.15\n- grey 0.22\n\nThe strongest positive association is yellow (0.34), and the strongest negative ones are green (-0.25) and black (-0.21).\n\n**Candidate:** It shows only 3 modules, with p-values in parentheses:\n- blue -0.12 (p=0.6)\n- turquoise -0.23 (p=0.3)\n- grey 0.045 (p=0.9)\n\n**Comparison:**\n- **Number of modules:** The candidate found 3 modules instead of 10, so module detection gave a very different result. The likely causes are a different soft-threshold power, minimum module size, merge cutoff, or preprocessing.\n- **Values for the shared modules:**\n  - Blue changes direction: +0.2 in the reference, -0.12 in the candidate.\n  - Turquoise has the same sign but a different size: -0.15 vs -0.23.\n  - Grey differs in size: 0.22 vs 0.045.\n- **Missing modules:** Yellow, pink, green and black, the reference's most disease-associated modules, do not appear in the candidate.\n- **Presentation:** The candidate includes p-values and the reference does not. Colorbar scales and styling also differ, but those are acceptable presentation differences.\n\nThe two plots are the same kind of figure, but the module structure and correlation values differ substantially. Their scientific message about which modules relate to disease is therefore different."
